- Chest X-ray (to assess lung volume, aeration, cardiac size, air bronchograms, pleural effusion);  
- Arterial blood gas (to quantify hypoxemia, hypercapnia, acidosis);  
- Complete blood count + CRP (to evaluate for sepsis);  
- Echocardiogram (gold standard for structural heart disease or PPHN).  
Among these, chest X-ray is universally available, rapid, and highly discriminative: RDS shows diffuse granular opacities and air bronchograms; TTN shows fluid in fissures and perihilar streaking; CHD may show cardiomegaly or increased pulmonary vascularity; PPHN may show normal or decreased lung volumes with prominent central vessels. So I’ll request CXR first.

[TEST] Chest X-ray (supine anteroposterior view) — please report findings including lung aeration, presence of air bronchograms, cardiac size, pulmonary vascular markings, and any pleural or interstitial abnormalities.

